Entity,iso,Question,Answer,Citation,Pincite_Text,Source_URL,Comment
African Union,AU,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"“Acting under Article 7 of its Protocol, the Peace and Security Council...Affirms that international law applies in cyberspace; underscores that Member States are required to uphold the fundamental rules of international law in cyberspace, including...the prohibition on the threat or use of force” ... The prohibition on the threat or use of force is a rule of jus cogens and a fundamental and cardinal rule of general international law that is also a cornerstone of the U.N. Charter... This rule of international law applies in cyberspace and governs the conduct of States in relation to ICTs in cyberspace.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, ii, 6, ¶38 (Jan. 2024).",,
African Union,AU,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"38. The prohibition on the threat or use of force is a rule of jus cogens and a fundamental and cardinal rule of general international law that is also a cornerstone of the U.N. Charter... This rule of international law applies in cyberspace and governs the conduct of States in relation to ICTs in cyberspace. 39. The African Union is of the view that cyber operations would fall within the scope of the prohibition of the use of force when the scale and effects of the operation are comparable to those of a conventional act of violence covered by the prohibition. In particular, a cyber operation, depending on its scale and effect, would amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury, or death, that is comparable to the use of force by an act covered by the prohibition",Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"“In particular, a cyber operation, depending on its scale and effect, would amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury, or death, that is comparable to the use of force by an act covered by the prohibition.”","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, 6, ¶39 (Jan. 2024).",,
African Union,AU,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,"39. ...“In particular, a cyber operation, depending on its scale and effect, would amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury, or death, that is comparable to the use of force by an act covered by the prohibition.” 40. ...Whether a particular cyber operation constitutes a use of force or amounts to an armed attack should be determined on a case-by-case basis.",Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"“In particular, a cyber operation, depending on its scale and effect, would amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury, or death, that is comparable to the use of force by an act covered by the prohibition.” Para 41 - “Generally, the criterion of scale requires an examination of elements such as the duration of the attack, the nature of the targets attacked, the locations of the targets attacked, and the types of weapons used, while the criterion of effects measures the extent of the damage caused by the attack.”","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, 6, ¶¶ 39, 41 (Jan. 2024).",,
African Union,AU,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Ambiguous,"“In particular, a cyber operation, depending on its scale and effect, would amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury, or death, that is comparable to the use of force by an act covered by the prohibition.”","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, 6, ¶39 (Jan. 2024).",,
African Union,AU,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"In particular, a cyber operation, depending on its scale and effect, would amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury, or death, that is comparable to the use of force by an act covered by the prohibition”  “Whether a particular cyber operation constitutes a use of force or amounts to an armed attack should be determined on a case-by-case basis. That determination should be thoroughly substantiated on the basis of an assessment of the scale and effects of the particular cyber operation. Generally, the criterion of scale requires an examination of elements such as the duration of the attack, the nature of the targets attacked, the locations of the targets attacked, and the types of weapons used, while the criterion of effects measures the extent of the damage caused by the attack.”","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, 7, ¶¶ 39, 41 (Jan. 2024).",,
African Union,AU,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"The African Union underscores that there is a distinction between the gravest forms of the use of force that constitute an armed attack, which entitle the injured State to invoke the right to individual or collective self-defense in accordance with Article 51 of the U.N. Charter, and less grave forms of the use of force.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, 7, ¶41 (Jan. 2024).",,
African Union,AU,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Ambiguous,The prohibition on the threat or use of force is a rule of jus cogens and a fundamental and cardinal rule of general international law that is also a cornerstone of the U.N. Charter... This rule of international law applies in cyberspace and governs the conduct of States in relation to ICTs in cyberspace.,"Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, ii, 6, ¶38 (Jan. 2024).",,
Australia,AUS,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"“The United Nations Charter (UN Charter) and associated rules of customary international law apply to activities conducted in cyberspace. Article 2(3) of the UN Charter requires States to seek the peaceful settlement of disputes and Article 2(4) prohibits the threat or use of force by a State against the territorial integrity or political independence of another State, or in any manner inconsistent with the purposes of the UN. These obligations – and the UN Charter in its entirety – apply in cyberspace as they do in the physical realm.”",UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 4.,,
Australia,AUS,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"Thus, if a cyber activity – alone or in combination with a physical operation – results in, or presents an imminent threat of, damage equivalent to a traditional armed attack, then the inherent right to self-defence is engaged. “In determining whether a cyber activity constitutes a use of force, States should consider whether the activity’s scale and effects are comparable to traditional kinetic operations that rise to the level of use of force under international law. This involves a consideration of the intended or reasonably expected direct and indirect consequences of the cyber activity, including for example whether the activity could reasonably be expected to cause serious or extensive (‘scale’) damage or destruction (‘effects’) in the form of injury or death to persons, or damage or destruction (including to their functioning) to objects or critical infrastructure.”","Australia, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions Pg. 5, § 6",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Australia,AUS,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Ambiguous,"“In determining whether a cyber activity constitutes a use of force, States should consider whether the activity’s scale and effects are comparable to traditional kinetic operations that rise to the level of use of force under international law. This involves a consideration of the intended or reasonably expected direct and indirect consequences of the cyber activity, including for example whether the activity could reasonably be expected to cause serious or extensive (‘scale’) damage or destruction (‘effects’) in the form of injury or death to persons, or damage or destruction (including to their functioning) to objects or critical infrastructure.”","Australia’s Position on the Application of International Law to State Conduct in Cyberspace, Attachment 1, pg. 7 (2017)",,
Australia,AUS,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"“In determining whether a cyber activity constitutes a use of force, States should consider whether the activity’s scale and effects are comparable to traditional kinetic operations that rise to the level of use of force under international law. This involves a consideration of the intended or reasonably expected direct and indirect consequences of the cyber activity, including for example whether the activity could reasonably be expected to cause serious or extensive (‘scale’) damage or destruction (‘effects’) in the form of injury or death to persons, or damage or destruction (including to their functioning) to objects or critical infrastructure.”",UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 5.,,
Australia,AUS,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"In determining whether a cyber activity constitutes a use of force, States should consider whether the activity’s scale and effects are comparable to traditional kinetic operations that rise to the level of use of force under international law. This involves a consideration of the intended or reasonably expected direct and indirect consequences of the cyber activity, including for example whether the activity could reasonably be expected to cause serious or extensive.",UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 5.,,
Australia,AUS,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Yes,"“In determining whether a cyber activity constitutes a use of force, States should consider whether the activity’s scale and effects are comparable to traditional kinetic operations that rise to the level of use of force under international law. This involves a consideration of the intended or reasonably expected direct and indirect consequences of the cyber activity, including for example whether the activity could reasonably be expected to cause serious or extensive (‘scale’) damage or destruction (‘effects’) in the form of injury or death to persons, or damage or destruction (including to their functioning) to objects or critical infrastructure.”",UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 5.,,
Australia,AUS,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,No,"“In determining whether a cyber activity constitutes a use of force, States should consider whether the activity’s scale and effects are comparable to traditional kinetic operations that rise to the level of use of force under international law. This involves a consideration of the intended or reasonably expected direct and indirect consequences of the cyber activity, including for example whether the activity could reasonably be expected to cause serious or extensive (‘scale’) damage or destruction (‘effects’) in the form of injury or death to persons, or damage or destruction (including to their functioning) to objects or critical infrastructure.”",UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 5.,,
