Australia,AUS,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Yes,...or destruction (including to their functioning) to objects or critical infrastructure.”,UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 5.,,
Australia,AUS,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Yes.,“...the intended or reasonably expected direct and indirect consequences of cyber activity...”,UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 5.,,
Australia,AUS,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,Ambiguous,“Australia considers that the thresholds and limitations governing the exercise of self-defence under Article 51 apply in respect of cyber activities that constitute an armed attack and in respect of acts of self-defence that are carried out by cyber means.”,UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 5.,,
Australia,AUS,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Yes,"The United Nations Charter (UN Charter) and associated rules of customary international law apply to activities conducted in cyberspace. Article 2(3) of the UN Charter requires States to seek the peaceful settlement of disputes and Article 2(4) prohibits the threat or use of force by a State against the territorial integrity or political independence of another State, or in any manner inconsistent with the purposes of the UN. These obligations – and the UN Charter in its entirety – apply in cyberspace as they do in the physical realm. ... Harmful conduct in cyberspace that does not constitute a use of force may constitute a breach of the duty not to intervene in the internal or external affairs of another State. This obligation is encapsulated in Article 2(7) of the Charter and in customary international law.",UNGA Compendium (2021) pp. 4-5.,,
Austria,AUT,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"Austria notes that while it is true that existing regimes of international law do not include explicit references to ICTs etc., it needs to be underlined that this does not mean that these rules do not apply in cyber space. Indeed, quite the contrary is the case: As stated by the GGE, existing international law in its entirety applies to cyber operations. ---------------------------------------------------------------------- The prohibition of the threat or use of force, as enshrined in Art. 2 para. 4 UN Charter, which is a jus cogens norm of customary international law, applies to cyber activities.","Pre-Draft Report of the OEWG – ICT, 2, ¶1 (Mar. 2020). ----------------------Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, 6, §5 (Apr. 2024).",,
Austria,AUT,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"A cyber activity may constitute a threat or use of force, and hence a violation of the UN Charter, if the scale and effects of the actual or threatened cyber activity are or would be comparable to those of a conduct using kinetic means.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, 6, §5 (Apr. 2024).",,
Austria,AUT,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,"Coercion is most evident in an intervention using force. However, also non-forcible forms of interference can be coercive.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, 5, §4 (Apr. 2024).",,
Austria,AUT,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"A cyber activity may constitute a threat or use of force, and hence a violation of the UN Charter, if the scale and effects of the actual or threatened cyber activity are or would be comparable to those of a conduct using kinetic means. ... A cyber activity constitutes a threat or use of force if its scale and effects are or would be comparable to those of a kinetic threat or use of force.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, 6, §5 (Apr. 2024).",,
Austria,AUT,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Ambiguous,"A cyber activity constitutes a threat or use of force if its scale and effects are or would be comparable to those of a kinetic threat or use of force. For instance, a cyber activity that leads to injury, death or significant physical damage constitutes an unlawful use of force. Cyber activities causing non-physical damage may also constitute an unlawful use of force. Moreover, a cyber activity could either be part of a wider operation using force with kinetic means, an independent use of force with physical effects, or an independent use of force without physical effects. (p. 7) A cyber operation constitutes an armed attack if, as in the kinetic context, it causes significant death or injury to persons, or substantial material damage or destruction. This is, however, also context-dependent and subject to a case-by-case analysis. An armed attack can also consist of a series of attacks","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, 6, §5 (Apr. 2024).",,
Austria,AUT,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"A cyber activity constitutes a threat or use of force if its scale and effects are or would be comparable to those of a kinetic threat or use of force. For instance, a cyber activity that leads to injury, death or significant physical damage constitutes an unlawful use of force. Cyber activities causing non-physical damage may also constitute an unlawful use of force. Moreover, a cyber activity could either be part of a wider operation using force with kinetic means, an independent use of force with physical effects, or an independent use of force without physical effects.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, 6, §5 (Apr. 2024).",,
Austria,AUT,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",No,"Cyber activities causing non-physical damage may also constitute an unlawful use of force. Moreover, a cyber activity could either be part of a wider operation using force with kinetic means, an independent use of force with physical effects, or an independent use of force without physical effects.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, 6, §5 (Apr. 2024).",,
Austria,AUT,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,No,"Cyber activities causing non-physical damage may also constitute an unlawful use of force. Moreover, a cyber activity could either be part of a wider operation using force with kinetic means, an independent use of force with physical effects, or an independent use of force without physical effects.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, 6, §5 (Apr. 2024).",,
Austria,AUT,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Yes,"Cyber activities causing non-physical damage may also constitute an unlawful use of force. Moreover, a cyber activity could either be part of a wider operation using force with kinetic means, an independent use of force with physical effects, or an independent use of force without physical effects.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, 6, §5 (Apr. 2024).",,
Austria,AUT,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Ambiguous,"An armed attack can also consist of a series of attacks.11 Likewise, while one cyber activity in isolation may not constitute an armed attack, several cyber activities may still constitute such an attack if, taken together, they are sufficiently grave to reach the threshold of an armed attack.",,,
Austria,AUT,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"A cyber activity may constitute an armed attack within the meaning of Art. 51 UN Charter whenever the scale and effects of the activity are comparable to those of an armed attack using kinetic means. ... Not every use of force constitutes an armed attack in the sense of Art. 51 UN Charter, both in the kinetic and the cyber context. An armed attack exists when force is used on a relatively large scale, is of sufficient gravity and has a substantial effect.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, 7, §6 (Apr. 2024).",,
Austria,AUT,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Yes,"A cyber activity may constitute a threat or use of force, and hence a violation of the UN Charter, if the scale and effects of the actual or threatened cyber activity are or would be comparable to those of a conduct using kinetic means.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, 6, §5 (Apr. 2024).",,
Belgium,BEL,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,A cyber operation could also be classified as a prohibited threat or use of force or armed attack.,"The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"A cyber operation could also be classified as a prohibited threat or use of force or armed attack. In all circumstances, it is the scale and effects of a cyber operation that must be observed in order to qualify as such.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,No,"A cyber operation could also be classified as a prohibited threat or use of force or armed attack. In all circumstances, it is the scale and effects of a cyber operation that must be observed in order to qualify as such.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,"Any use of force against a State that results in death, injury or significant physical destruction is covered by the rule. However, it cannot be ruled out that a cyber operation that does not result in significant physical or human damage could be considered as a prohibited use of force, provided that its scale and effects are similar to those resulting from the use of other weapons and military means.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"Consequently, if the scale and effects of a cyber operation are similar to those resulting from the use of other weapons or military means (i.e. kinetic means), the concerned cyber operation can be considered as a prohibited threat or use of force.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,Whether the threshold for concluding that force has been used unlawfully has been exceeded will always depend on a case-by-case analysis by the competent authorities of the victim State.,"The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"Any use of force against a State that results in death, injury or significant physical destruction is covered by the rule.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",No,"Any use of force against a State that results in death, injury or significant physical destruction is covered by the rule. However, it cannot be ruled out that a cyber operation that does not result in significant physical or human damage could be considered as a prohibited use of force, provided that its scale and effects are similar to those resulting from the use of other weapons and military means. This could be the case of a cyber operation deliberately seeking to affect a State, by paralysing its financial system or the operation of its critical/essential infrastructures, with serious consequences for the health, safety or economic well-being of the population.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,No,"Any use of force against a State that results in death, injury or significant physical destruction is covered by the rule. However, it cannot be ruled out that a cyber operation that does not result in significant physical or human damage could be considered as a prohibited use of force, provided that its scale and effects are similar to those resulting from the use of other weapons and military means. This could be the case of a cyber operation deliberately seeking to affect a State, by paralysing its financial system or the operation of its critical/essential infrastructures, with serious consequences for the health, safety or economic well-being of the population.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Yes,"Any use of force against a State that results in death, injury or significant physical destruction is covered by the rule. However, it cannot be ruled out that a cyber operation that does not result in significant physical or human damage could be considered as a prohibited use of force, provided that its scale and effects are similar to those resulting from the use of other weapons and military means. This could be the case of a cyber operation deliberately seeking to affect a State, by paralysing its financial system or the operation of its critical/essential infrastructures, with serious consequences for the health, safety or economic well-being of the population.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Ambiguous,"Under the theory of cumulative events, however, several events which, taken in isolation, could not be considered as armed attack, could, when taken cumulatively (with due regard to links in time, source and cause), reach the threshold of seriousness required to give rise to the right to self-defence.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"For an unlawful use of force between States to be classified as an armed attack, a higher threshold will be required for the right of self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter. The attack must, at the very least, result in substantial loss of life or extensive destruction of infrastructure or systems (at least when the destruction affects vital or military infrastructure) in order to qualify as such and give rise to the right to individual or collective self-defence.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Yes,A cyber operation could also be classified as a prohibited threat or use of force or armed attack.,"The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Bolivia,BOL,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"“it can be stated that the areas of current international law are applicable to  CYBERSPACE” “In analyzing and comparing the two laws, it can be inferred that: a CYBER OPERATION constitutes use of force and generates the right to legitimate defense, based on Art. 51 of the United Nations Charter.”","Boliva, OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire, § 3A, 4A (2019).",,
Bolivia,BOL,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"“In analyzing and comparing the two laws, it can be inferred that: a CYBER OPERATION constitutes use of force and generates the right to legitimate defense, based on Art. 51 of the United Nations Charter.”","Boliva, OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire, § 4A (2019).",,
Bolivia,BOL,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"“because the effects of cyber-attacks will not always be immediately known, hence it will not be easy to check “whether use of force” was resorted to which determines whether there is a “self-defense” response.,”","Boliva, OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire, § 4A (2019).",,
Bolivia,BOL,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"This statement is more genuinely justified when the “use of force” arises in a cyber-attack considered as an “armed attack,” on the understanding that a cyber virtual attack uses unconventional means that have the same impact an armed attack” however “the effects of cyber-attacks will not always be immediately known, hence it will not be easy to check “whether use of force” was resorted to…”","Boliva, OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire, § 4A (2019).",,
Bolivia,BOL,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
