Cuba,CUB,4. Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
Cuba,CUB,5. Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Silent,"6. The concept of the use of force has been analyzed from a perspective that involves the notions of armed violence, coercive force or force of interference...Under current international law, a cyber action does not constitute an armed attack in accordance with Article 2(4), since it lacks the physical characteristics and other requirements that define the aforementioned military attacks, their effects and legal consequences.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc=%7BCF77EE55-EC10-4A8A-88B3-FF719D0EC527%7D&file=Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx&action=default&mobileredirect=true,
Cuba,CUB,"6. Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Silent,,,,
Cuba,CUB,6.1 Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Silent,"12. Armed attacks, as regulated in Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations, are the only action that justifies the exercise of self-defense as an exception to the jus cogens prohibition on the use of force. In this context, the use of force can only be understood as the use of the armed forces of one State against another and not the use of any type of force, whether economic, commercial, financial, cyber, or verbal.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc=%7BCF77EE55-EC10-4A8A-88B3-FF719D0EC527%7D&file=Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx&action=default&mobileredirect=true,
Cuba,CUB,6.2 Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Cuba,CUB,"6.3 Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",No,"12. Armed attacks, as regulated in Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations, are the only action that justifies the exercise of self-defense as an exception to the jus cogens prohibition on the use of force. In this context, the use of force can only be understood as the use of the armed forces of one State against another and not the use of any type of force, whether economic, commercial, financial, cyber, or verbal.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc=%7BCF77EE55-EC10-4A8A-88B3-FF719D0EC527%7D&file=Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx&action=default&mobileredirect=true,
Cuba,CUB,"6.4 Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Cuba,CUB,7. Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Cuba,CUB,8. Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,“...the definition of armed attack is much more restrictive than that of use of force... a cyber action does not constitute an armed attack... ” “Armed attacks... are the only action that justifies the exercise of self-defense as an exception to the jus cogens prohibition on the use of force” “It is politically dangerous and legally premature to consider that an improper use of ICTs could be equated to the use of force for the purpose of triggering the recourse to self-defense...”,Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc=%7BCF77EE55-EC10-4A8A-88B3-FF719D0EC527%7D&file=Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx&action=default&mobileredirect=true,
Cuba,CUB,9. Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"25. The prohibition of the threat or use of force contained in the UN Charter is deliberately general and encompasses any means and methods used (whether kinetic or cyber means). Thus, the prohibition of the threat or use of force applies also in cyberspace.","2024 Czech Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Czech%20Republic/2024%20Czech%20Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=cIyRBp,
Czech Republic,CZE,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,26. ...The Czech Republic is of the opinion that cyber operation conducted in cyberspace could amount to the use of force under Article 2(4) of the UN Charter when the effects of the operation are comparable to those of a conventional character.,"2024 Czech Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Czech%20Republic/2024%20Czech%20Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=cIyRBp,
Czech Republic,CZE,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Ambiguous.,"27... Whether activities in cyberspace, attributable to a State under international law, violate the prohibition of the threat or  use of force as contained in Article 2(4) of the UN Charter needs to be assessed on case-by-case  basis. Activities conducted by cyber means that do not amount to a threat or use of force may still amount to a violation of sovereignty or a prohibited intervention into internal or external affairs. In the context of cyber operations, the Czech Republic is of the view that factors offered by the Tallinn Manual 2.0, such as severity, immediacy, directness, invasiveness, measurability of effects, military character, State involvement or presumptive legality of the cyber operation in question represent important criteria in the process of evaluation when deciding whether an act may be characterized as an unlawful use of force. 29. ...In accordance with the general guidance provided by the International Court of Justice there are two main factors to be considered when assessing whether a use of force constitutes an armed attack: the scale and the effects of the particular act.  If a cyber operation was comparable in its scale and effect to an attack by conventional means (kinetic operations) in terms of its gravity, such as fatalities, damage and destruction, it could be considered as constituting an armed attack under Article 51 of the UN Charter.","2024 Czech Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Czech%20Republic/2024%20Czech%20Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=cIyRBp,
Czech Republic,CZE,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,"27. ...Whether activities in cyberspace, attributable to a State under international law, violate the prohibition of the threat or use of force as contained in Article 2(4) of the UN Charter needs to be assessed on case-by-case basis. Activities conducted by cyber means that do not amount to a threat or use of force may still amount to a violation of sovereignty or a prohibited intervention into internal or external affairs. In the context of cyber operations, the Czech Republic is of the view that factors offered by the Tallinn Manual 2.0, such as severity, immediacy, directness, invasiveness, measurability of effects, military character, State involvement or presumptive legality of the cyber operation in question represent important criteria in the process of evaluation when deciding whether an act may be characterized as an unlawful use of force.","2024 Czech Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Czech%20Republic/2024%20Czech%20Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=cIyRBp,
Czech Republic,CZE,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"26 ... The Czech Republic is of the opinion that cyber  operation conducted in cyberspace could amount to the use of force under Article 2(4) of the UN Charter when the effects of the operation are comparable to those of a conventional character  27. ...Whether activities in cyberspace, attributable to a State under international law, violate the prohibition of the threat or use of force as contained in Article 2(4) of the UN Charter needs to be assessed on case-by-case basis. ... In the context of cyber operations, the Czech Republic is of the view that factors offered by the Tallinn Manual 2.0, such as severity, immediacy, directness, invasiveness, measurability of effects, military character, State involvement or presumptive legality of the cyber operation in question represent important criteria in the process of evaluation when deciding whether an act may be characterized as an unlawful use of force.","2024 Czech Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Czech%20Republic/2024%20Czech%20Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=cIyRBp,
Czech Republic,CZE,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Ambiguous,"If a cyber operation was comparable in its scale and effect to an attack by conventional means (kinetic operations) in terms of its gravity, such as fatalities, damage and destruction, it could be considered as constituting an armed attack under Article 51 of the UN Charter.","2024 Czech Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Czech%20Republic/2024%20Czech%20Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=cIyRBp,
Czech Republic,CZE,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"If a cyber operation was comparable in its scale and effect to an attack by conventional means (kinetic operations) in terms of its gravity, such as fatalities, damage and destruction, it could be considered as constituting an armed attack under Article 51 of the UN Charter.","2024 Czech Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Czech%20Republic/2024%20Czech%20Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=cIyRBp,
Czech Republic,CZE,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Ambiguous,"26. Given  the  dependency  of  all  States  and  societies  on  ICTs,  cyber activities can have  serious consequences not only in cyberspace but also in the physical world. Such activities can result in malfunction of critical  infrastructure, physical damage  or extensive  economic loss, threaten national security or put human lives into jeopardy. The Czech Republic is of the opinion that cyber operation conducted in cyberspace could amount to the use of force under Article 2(4) of the UN Charter when the effects of the operation are comparable to those of a conventional character.","2024 Czech Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Czech%20Republic/2024%20Czech%20Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=cIyRBp,
Czech Republic,CZE,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"28. Depending on the facts and circumstances, cyber operations attributable to a State and amounting to an unlawful use of force under Article 2(4) of the UN Charter may also constitute an ""armed attack"", under Article 51 of the UN Charter.","2024 Czech Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Czech%20Republic/2024%20Czech%20Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=cIyRBp,
Czech Republic,CZE,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Yes,"25. The prohibition of the threat or use of force contained in the UN Charter is deliberately general and encompasses any means and methods used (whether kinetic or cyber means). Thus, the prohibition of the threat or use of force applies also in cyberspace.","2024 Czech Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Czech%20Republic/2024%20Czech%20Republic_Position_paper_on_the_application_of_IL_cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=cIyRBp,
Denmark,DNK,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"“Cyber operations may violate the prohibition on the threat or use of force, which primarily depends on the physical scale and effects of the cyber operation in question.”","National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, (2023) pg. 450.",,
Denmark,DNK,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"Cyber operations may violate the prohibition on the threat or use of force, which primarily depends on the physical scale and effects of the cyber operation in question.","National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, (2023) pg. 450.",,
Denmark,DNK,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Yes,"P. 450: As emphasized by the Court in the  Nicaragua judgment coercive acts involving the use of force are particularly obvious examples of unlawful interventions.4 Denmark considers that coercion is not limited to means of direct or indirect use of force and that also  measures below this threshold may constitute coercion. Cyber activities that  do not amount to use of force can therefore also be coercive  With regard to the precise interpretation of the term force and the question as to whether economic or political coercion can qualify as use of force, Denmark considers that it generally cannot be ruled out that acts of economic or political coercion can fall within the purview of Article 2(4) of the UN Charter if, for example, a cyber operation resulting in the malfunctioning of a State’s financial system leads to significant economic damage.","National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, (2023) pg. 451.",,
Denmark,DNK,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"Cyber operations may violate the prohibition on the threat or use of force, which primarily depends on the physical scale and effects of the cyber operation in question. It requires an individual assessment of the specific circumstances in each case to determine whether the scale and effects of a cyber operation correspond to what would qualify as use of force had they resulted from conventional weapons.","National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, (2023) pg. 450.",,
Denmark,DNK,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,"It requires an individual assessment of the specific circumstances in each case to determine whether the scale and effects of a cyber operation correspond to what would qualify as use of force had they resulted from conventional weapons. It has been suggested that States should apply the following non-exhaustive factors for determining if a cyber operation reaches the level of use of force: Severity, immediacy, directness, invasiveness, measurability of effects, military character, State involvement, presumptive legality.[5] While few States in their public positions have endorsed these particular factors, Denmark is of the view that these factors are useful reference points for further understanding and discussing the definition of use of force in cyberspace.","National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, (2023) pg. 451.",,
Denmark,DNK,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"Cyber operations may violate the prohibition on the threat or use of force, which primarily depends on the physical scale and effects of the cyber operation in question. It requires an individual assessment of the specific circumstances in each case to determine whether the scale and effects of a cyber operation correspond to what would qualify as use of force had they resulted from conventional weapons.","National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, (2023) pg. 450-51.",,
Denmark,DNK,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",No,"Cyber operations may violate the prohibition on the threat or use of force, which primarily depends on the physical scale and effects of the cyber operation in question. It requires an individual assessment of the specific circumstances in each case to determine whether the scale and effects of a cyber operation correspond to what would qualify as use of force had they resulted from conventional weapons. Generally, Denmark subscribes to the notion that where a cyber operation results in injury, death, or significant physical damage, this prima facie qualifies as use of force. 451: With regard to the precise interpretation of the term force and the question as to whether economic or political coercion can qualify as use of force, Denmark considers that it generally cannot be ruled out that acts of economic or political coercion can fall within the purview of Article 2(4) of the UN  Charter if, for example, a cyber operation resulting in the malfunctioning of a  State’s financial system leads to significant economic damage.","National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, (2023) pg. 450-51.",,
Denmark,DNK,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,No,"It has been suggested that States should apply the following non-exhaustive factors for determining if a cyber operation reaches the level of use of force: Severity, immediacy, directness, invasiveness, measurability of effects, military character, State involvement, presumptive legality. While few States in their public positions have endorsed these particular factors, Denmark is of the view that these factors are useful reference points for further understanding and discussing the definition of use of force in cyberspace.","National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, (2023) pg. 451.",,
Denmark,DNK,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Yes,"451: With regard to the precise interpretation of the term force and the question as to whether economic or political coercion can qualify as use of force, Denmark considers that it generally cannot be ruled out that acts of economic or political coercion can fall within the purview of Article 2(4) of the UN  Charter if, for example, a cyber operation resulting in the malfunctioning of a  State’s financial system leads to significant economic damage.","National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, (2023) pg. 451.",,
Denmark,DNK,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Yes,"“Denmark considers that a cyber operation, which e.g. leads to serious injury or death, or which causes significant physical damage, may qualify as an armed attack. This could be the case if a cyber attack leads to the disabling of an air traffic control system which causes planes to crash or an interference with the operating system of a power station, which causes serious physical damage.”","National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, pg. 452 (2023)",,
Denmark,DNK,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,Denmark subscribes to the understanding that not all illegal use of force under article 2(4) of the UN Charter necessarily amounts to an armed attack under article 51 of the Charter.,"National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, (2023) pg. 451-52.",,
Denmark,DNK,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Yes,"Cyber operations may violate the prohibition on the threat or use of force, which primarily depends on the physical scale and effects of the cyber operation in question.","National position of Denmark, Applicability of International Law, (2023) pg. 450.",,
Ecuador,ECU,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,“Yes. The fields of international law do apply in cyberspace.”,"Permanent Mission of Ecuador, Questions and Answers RE Note Verbale, Question 2 (2019).",,
Ecuador,ECU,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Ecuador,ECU,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Ambiguous,"“According to NATO, a cyber attack is ‘an offensive or defensive cyber operation that is intended to cause injury or kill people or to damage or destroy objects.’ According to this concept, it can be considered an armed attack”. (question 3)",,,
