Ecuador,ECU,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"States must refrain in their international relations from carrying out cyber operations which, based on their scale and effect, would constitute a threat or use of force against the territorial integrity or a political independence of any state, or in any other manner inconsistent with the purposes of the UN.","Estonia, UNGA Compendium, pg. 25 (2021)",,
Estonia,EST,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"A cyber operation that targets critical infrastructure and results in serious damage, injury or death, or a threat of such an operation, would be an example of use of force.","Estonia, UNGA Compendium, pg. 26 (2021)",,
Estonia,EST,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"“States must refrain in their international relations from carrying out cyber operations which, based on their scale and effect, would constitute a threat or use of force against the territorial integrity or a political independence of any state, or in any other manner inconsistent with the purposes of the UN.” In order to assess if a cyber operation reaches the threshold of the use of force or an armed attack based on Article 2(4) or 51 of the UN Charter, we must consider the scale and effects of the operation.","Estonia, UNGA Compendium, pg. 25, 30 (2021)",,
Estonia,EST,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"A cyber operation that targets critical infrastructure and results in serious damage, injury or death, or a threat of such an operation, would be an example of use of force.","Estonia, UNGA Compendium, pg. 26 (2021)",,
Estonia,EST,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Ambiguous,"“A cyber operation that targets critical infrastructure and results in serious damage, injury or death, or a threat of such an operation, would be an example of use of force.""","Estonia, UNGA Compendium, pg. 26 (2021)",,
Estonia,EST,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"States must refrain in their international relations from carrying out cyber operations which, based on their scale and effect, would constitute a threat or use of force against the territorial integrity or a political independence of any state, or in any other manner inconsistent with the purposes of the UN.",,,
Estonia,EST,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",S Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,Ambiguous,"In order to assess if a cyber operation reaches the threshold of the use of force or an armed attack based on Article 2(4) or 51 of the UN Charter, we must consider the scale and effects of the operation.","Estonia, UNGA Compendium, pg. 30",,
Estonia,EST,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Yes,"A cyber operation that targets critical infrastructure and results in serious damage, injury or death, or a threat of such an operation, would be an example of use of force.","Estonia, UNGA Compendium, pg. 25 (2021)",,
European Union,EUN,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"“Modern societies have come to rely heavily on ICT which can be disrupted, its usage prevented or denied or its workings altered by a cyber operation without resulting in any physical damage. A cyber operation can have far-reaching adverse effects or cause severe interruption of daily life. Depending on the circumstances, the combined effects of several cyber operations could, taken together, be comparable to a kinetic use of force.” “Whenever the scale and effects of a cyber operation are comparable to those of a traditional kinetic use of force, it constitutes a use of force within the meaning of Article 2 (4) of the UN Charter, which is prohibited unless specifically justified.”","European Union (Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace (2024), page 6",,
European Union,EUN,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"“Whenever the scale and effects of a cyber operation are comparable to those of a traditional kinetic use of force, it constitutes a use of force within the meaning of Article 2 (4) of the UN Charter, which is prohibited unless specifically justified.”","European Union (Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace (2024), page 6",,
European Union,EUN,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"“Whenever the scale and effects of a cyber operation are comparable to those of a traditional kinetic use of force, it constitutes a use of force within the meaning of Article 2 (4) of the UN Charter, which is prohibited unless specifically justified.”","European Union (Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace (2024), page 6",,
European Union,EUN,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Ambiguous,"“Whenever the scale and effects of a cyber operation are comparable to those of a traditional kinetic use of force, it constitutes a use of force within the meaning of Article 2 (4) of the UN Charter, which is prohibited unless specifically justified.”","European Union (Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace (2024), page 6",,
European Union,EUN,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"“A cyber operation can have far-reaching adverse effects or cause severe interruption of daily life.  Depending on the circumstances, the combined effects of several cyber operations could, taken together, be comparable to a kinetic use of force. Whenever the scale and effects of a cyber operation are comparable to those of a traditional kinetic use of force, it constitutes a use of force within the meaning of Article 2 (4) of the UN Charter, which is prohibited unless specifically justified.”","European Union (Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace (2024), page 6",,
European Union,EUN,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Yes,"Depending on the circumstances, the combined effects of several cyber operations could, taken together, be comparable to a kinetic use of force.","European Union (Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace (2024), page 6",,
European Union,EUN,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"“The use of force that takes place within the limits of the consent of the State in whose territory that force will be used does not violate the prohibition on the use of force. Moreover, the use of force does not always constitute an armed attack.”","European Union (Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace (2024), page 6",,
European Union,EUN,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"While there is currently no established definition of a cyberattack that would pass the threshold of “use of force” in the sense of article 2(4) of the UN Charter, or “armed attack” in the sense of article 51, it is widely recognized that such a qualification depends on the consequences of a  cyberattack. For a cyberattack to be comparable to use of force, it must be sufficiently serious and have impacts in the territory of the target State, or in areas within its jurisdiction, that are similar to those of the use of force.","Finland Position (2020), pg 6",,
Finland,FIN,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"While there is currently no established definition of a cyberattack that would pass the threshold of “use of force” in the sense of article 2(4) of the UN Charter, or “armed attack” in the sense of  article 51, it is widely recognized that such a qualification depends on the consequences of a cyberattack. For a cyberattack to be comparable to use of force, it must be sufficiently serious and have impacts in the territory of the target State, or in areas within its jurisdiction, that are similar to those of the use of force.","Finland Position (2020), pg 6",,
Finland,FIN,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Yes,"According to the International Court of Justice, the element of coercion is particularly clear if  force is used through means of military action, threats of such action, or through support to  armed groups in another State","Finland Position (2020), pg 3,",,
Finland,FIN,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Ambiguous,"Most commentators agree that when the scale and effects of a cyberattack correspond to those of an armed attack responding to the cyberattack is justifiable as self-defence. It is obvious that the attack must have caused death, injury or substantial material damage, but it is impossible to set a precise quantitative threshold for the effects, and other circumstantial factors must be taken into account in the analysis, as well (page 6)","Finland Position (2020), pg 6, para 4/second to last full para",,
Finland,FIN,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Ambiguous,"Most commentators agree that when the scale and effects of a cyberattack correspond to those of an armed attack responding to the cyberattack is justifiable as self-defence. It is obvious that the attack must have caused death, injury or substantial material damage, but it is impossible to set a precise quantitative threshold for the effects, and other circumstantial factors must be taken into account in the analysis, as well (page 6)","Finland Position (2020), pg 6, para 4/second to last full para",,
Finland,FIN,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"A widely discussed question is, to what extent the definition of a cyberattack comparable to an armed attack should take account of the indirectand long-term impacts of the attack. In any case, this would require that the impacts can beassessed with sufficient precision","Finland Position (2020), pg 6, para 5/last full para",,
Finland,FIN,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"For a cyberattack to be comparable to use of force, it must be sufficiently serious and have impacts in the territory of the target State, or in areas within its jurisdiction, that aresimilar to those of the use of force. (page 6)","Finland Position (2020), pg 6, para 4/second to last full para",,
Finland,FIN,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Ambiguous,"A widely discussed question is, to what extent  the definition of a cyberattack comparable to an armed attack should take account of the indirect and long-term impacts of the attack. In any case, this would require that the impacts can be assessed with sufficient precision.","Finland Position (2020), pg 6, para 5",,
Finland,FIN,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"While there is currently no established definition of a cyberattack that would pass the thresholdof “use of force” in the sense of article 2(4) of the UN Charter, or “armed attack” in the sense of article 51, it is widely recognized that such a qualification depends on the consequences of a cyberattack. Any interpretation of the use of force in cyberspace should respect the UN Charter and not just the letter of the Charter but also its object and purpose, which is to prevent the escalation of armed activities. This would mean, for instance, that the distinction between armed attack as a particularly serious violation of the Charter, on the one hand, and any lesser uses of force, on the other, is preserved.","Finland Position (2020), pg 6-7",,
Finland,FIN,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Yes,"A threat of such a cyberattack could also violate Article 2(4) of the Charter, if the threat is sufficiently precise and directed against another State. (page 6)","Finland Position (2020), pg 6, para 4/second to last full para",,
France,FRA,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"The most serious violations of sovereignty, especially those that infringe France’s territorial integrity or political independence, may violate the prohibition of the threat or use of force, which applies to any use of force, regardless of the weapons employed.","France, International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace, pg. 3, § 1.1.2 (2021).",,
France,FRA,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"Some cyberoperations may violate the prohibition of the threat or use of force. The most serious violations of sovereignty, especially those that infringe France’s territorial integrity or political independence, may violate the prohibition of the threat or use of force, which applies to any use of force, regardless of the weapons employed.","France, International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace, pg. 3, § 1.1.2 (2021).",,
France,FRA,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,"A cyberoperation may be deemed a use of force against the yardstick of several criteria, including the circumstances prevailing at the time of the operation, such as the origin of the operation and the nature of the instigator (military or not), the extent of intrusion, the actual or intended effects of the operation or the nature of the intended target. This is of course not an exhaustive list.","France, International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace, pg. 3, § 1.1.2 (2021).",,
France,FRA,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Ambiguous,A cyberattack could be categorised as an armed attack if it caused substantial loss of life or considerable physical or economic damage. That would be the case of an operation in cyberspace that caused a failure of critical infrastructure with significant consequences or consequences liable to paralyse whole swathes of the country’s activity . . .,"France, International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace, pg. 3, § 1.2.1 (2021).",,
France,FRA,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",No,"However, France does not rule out the possibility that a cyberoperation without physical effects may also be characterised as a use of force. In the absence of physical damage, a cyberoperation may be deemed a use of force against the yardstick of several criteria . . .","France, International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace, pg. 3, § 1.1.2 (2021).",,
France,FRA,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,No,"A cyberoperation carried out by one State against another State violates the prohibition of the use of force if its effects are similar to those that result from the use of conventional weapons. However, France does not rule out the possibility that a cyberoperation without physical effects may also be characterised as a use of force. In the absence of physical damage, a cyberoperation may be deemed a use of force against the yardstick of several criteria, including the circumstances prevailing at the time of the operation, such as the origin of the operation and the nature of the instigator (military or not), the extent of intrusion, the actual or intended effects of the operation or the nature of the intended target. This is of course not an exhaustive list. For example, penetrating military systems in order to compromise French defence capabilities, or financing or even training individuals to carry out cyberattacks against France, could also be deemed uses of force. (para 1.1.2)","France, International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace, pg. 3-4, § 1.1.2 (2021).",,
France,FRA,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Yes,"This is of course not an exhaustive list. For example, penetrating military systems in order to compromise French defence capabilities, or financing or even training individuals to carry out cyberattacks against France, could also be deemed uses of force. (para 1.1.2)","France, International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace, pg. 3-4, § 1.1.2 (2021).",,
France,FRA,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
France,FRA,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Ambiguous,"Cyberattacks which do not reach the threshold of an armed attack when taken in isolation could be categorised as such if the accumulation of their effects reaches a sufficient threshold of gravity, or if they are carried out concurrently with operations in the physical sphere which constitute an armed attack, where such attacks are coordinated and stem from the same entity or from different entities acting in Concert. (para 1.2.2)","France, International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace, pg. 7, § 1.2.2 (2021).",,
France,FRA,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"However, not every use of force is an armed attack within the meaning of Article 51 of the United Nations Charter, especially if its effects are limited or reversible or do not attain a certain level of gravity.  France reaffirms that a cyberattack may constitute an armed attack within the meaning of Article 51 of the United Nations Charter19, if it is of a scale and severity comparable to those resulting from the use of physical force (para. 1.2.1)","France, International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace, pg. 4, § 1.1.2 (2021).",,
France,FRA,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Yes,Some cyberoperations may violate the prohibition of the threat or use of force (para 1.1.2),"France, International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace, pg. 3, § 1.1.2 (2021).",,
Germany,DEU,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"So far, the vast majority of malicious cyber operations fall outside the scope of ‘force’. However, cyber operations might in extremis fall within the scope of the prohibition of the use of force and thus constitute a breach of art. 2 para. 4 UN Charter.","Germany Position Paper (2021), pg. 6, II.c",,
Germany,DEU,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"Cyber operations can cross the threshold into use of force and cause significant damage in two ways. Firstly, they can be part of a wider kinetic attack. In such cases they are one component of a wider operation clearly involving the use of physical force, and can be assessed within the examination of the wider incident. Secondly, outside the wider context of a kinetic military operation, cyber operations can by themselves cause serious harm and may result in massive casualties. (Page 6)","Germany Position Paper (2021), pg. 6, II.c,",,
Germany,DEU,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Ambiguous,"In its Nicaragua judgement, the International Court of Justice (ICJ) held that ‘[t]he element of coercion, which defines, and indeed forms the very essence of, prohibited intervention, is particularly obvious in the case of an intervention which uses force, either in the direct form of military action, or in the indirect form of support for subversive or terrorist armed activities within another State.’ 73 Malicious cyber activities will only in some cases amount to direct or indirect use of force. 74 However, measures below this threshold may also qualify as coercive","Germany Position Paper (2021), pg. 5,",,
Germany,DEU,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"With regard to the latter case, Germany shares the view expressed in the Tallinn Manual 2.0: the threshold of use of force in cyber operations is defined, in analogy to the ICJ’s Nicaragua judgement, by the scale and effects of such a cyber operation. Whenever scale and effects of a cyber operation are comparable to those of a traditional kinetic use of force, it would constitute a breach of art. 2 para. 4 UN Charter. (page 6)","Germany Position Paper (2021), pg. 6, II.c, para 4-5",,
Germany,DEU,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,"Based on the assessment of the scale and effects of the operation, the broader context of the situation and the significance of the malicious cyber operation will have to be taken into account. Qualitative criteria which may play a role in the assessment are, inter alia, the severity of the interference, the immediacy of its effects, the degree of intrusion into a foreign cyber infrastructure and the degree of organization and coordination of the malicious cyber operation. (page 6)","Germany Position Paper (2021), pg. 6, II.c, last para",,
Germany,DEU,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"Based on the assessment of the scale and effects of the operation, the broader context of the situation and the significance of the malicious cyber operation will have to be taken into account. Qualitative criteria which may play a role in the assessment are, inter alia, the severity of the interference, the immediacy of its effects, the degree of intrusion into a foreign cyber infrastructure and the degree of organization and coordination of the malicious cyber operation. (page 6)","Germany Position Paper (2021), pg. 6, II.c, last para",,
Germany,DEU,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Yes,"Whenever scale and effects of a cyber operation are comparable to those of a traditional kinetic use of force, it would constitute a breach of art. 2 para. 4 UN Charter  The determination of a cyber operation as having crossed the threshold of a prohibited use of force is a decision to be taken on a case-by-case basis. Based on the assessment of the scale and effects of the operation, the broader context of the situation and the significance of the malicious cyber operation will have to be taken into account. Qualitative criteria which may play a role in the assessment are, inter alia, the severity of the interference, the immediacy of its effects, the degree of intrusion into a foreign cyber infrastructure and the degree of organization and coordination of the malicious cyber operation (page 6)","Germany Position Paper (2021), pg. 6, II.c",,
Germany,DEU,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,),"Germany Position Paper (2021), pg. 6, II.c, para 4",,
Germany,DEU,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"Furthermore, Germany acknowledges the view expressed in the ICJ’s Nicaragua judgment, namely that an armed attack constitutes the gravest form of use of force.","Germany Position Paper (2021), pg. 15, IV.b.4, para 4",,
Germany,DEU,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Guyana,GUY,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Ambiguous,"It is respectfully submitted that while it is acknowledged that international law should or ought to apply to cyberspace, given the nature of cyberspace and non-physical elements of attacks and consequences, it is difficult to easily apply existing principles of international law, for example with the “use of force”, where it traditionally implies some physical element and armed attacks which traditionally imply some sort od weapon. In the cyberspace, these cyber oprations do not fit into traditional concepts. (Question 2)","Permanent Mission of the Republic of Guyana to the Organization of American States, Note No: 105/2019, pg. 2, Answer 2 (2019).",,
Guyana,GUY,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,No,"A cyber operation therefore covers the entire scope of cyberspace and related operations, both technical and non-technical. According to Black’s law dictionary, force is “Power dynamically considered, that is , in motion or in action”. Use of force relates to the extent to which this power is used. Consequently, a cyber operation by itself may not constitute a use of force. (Question 3)","Permanent Mission of the Republic of Guyana to the Organization of American States, Note No: 105/2019, pg. 3, Answer 3 2019).",,
