Guatemala,GTM,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,Question 3: Yes: a cyber operation in and of itself can qualify as a use of force.,"OAS Survey Response Submitted by Guatemala, pg3, question 3.",,
Guatemala,GTM,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,Question 3: Yes: a cyber operation in and of itself can qualify as a use of force.,"OAS Survey Response Submitted by Guatemala, pg3, question 3.",,
Guatemala,GTM,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Guatemala,GTM,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Guatemala,GTM,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Guatemala,GTM,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
Guatemala,GTM,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"Question 3: ...Thought must be given to expanding our concept of the term “weapon,” in order to see them in the light of their consequences and not that of the physical components with which they are built. In light of the foregoing, we believe that there is a right of legitimate defense against a cyber attack or operation against a country’s sovereignty. This is because the consequences thereof can be greater and more lasting, in that they threaten such sectors as health, security, and others. In addition, because of the consequences they can produce — such as death, anxiety, and poverty — they should be considered violent.","OAS Survey Response Submitted by Guatemala, pg3, question 3.",,
Guatemala,GTM,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",No,"Question 3: “use of force” does not exclusively mean physical force; it also covers threats to and violations of the security and protection of third parties. Although they are not weapons as we commonly understand them, cyber channels can be used to deploy weapons or measures with a similar — or even greater — impact than that obtained with kinetic weapons. Thought must be given to expanding our concept of the term “weapon,” in order to see them in the light of their consequences and not that of the physical components with which they are built. In light of the foregoing, we believe that there is a right of legitimate defense against a cyber attack or operation against a country’s sovereignty. This is because the consequences thereof can be greater and more lasting, in that they threaten such sectors as health, security, and others. In addition, because of the consequences they can produce — such as death, anxiety, and poverty — they should be considered violent.","OAS Survey Response Submitted by Guatemala, pg3, question 3.",,
Guatemala,GTM,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,No,"Question 3: “use of force” does not exclusively mean physical force; it also covers threats to and violations of the security and protection of third parties. Although they are not weapons as we commonly understand them, cyber channels can be used to deploy weapons or measures with a similar — or even greater — impact than that obtained with kinetic weapons. Thought must be given to expanding our concept of the term “weapon,” in order to see them in the light of their consequences and not that of the physical components with which they are built. In light of the foregoing, we believe that there is a right of legitimate defense against a cyber attack or operation against a country’s sovereignty. This is because the consequences thereof can be greater and more lasting, in that they threaten such sectors as health, security, and others. In addition, because of the consequences they can produce — such as death, anxiety, and poverty — they should be considered violent.","OAS Survey Response Submitted by Guatemala, pg3, question 3.",,
Guatemala,GTM,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Guatemala,GTM,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Silent,,,,
Guatemala,GTM,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Guatemala,GTM,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Guatemala,GTM,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,Silent,,,,
Guatemala,GTM,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Ambiguous,"Question 3: Yes: a cyber operation in and of itself can qualify as a use of force, since “use of force” does not exclusively mean physical force; it also covers threats to and violations of the security and protection of third parties.","OAS Survey Response Submitted by Guatemala, pg3, question 3.",,
Iran,IRN,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"Considering that a wide range of general principles of current international law, including equality of sovereignty of states, the prohibition against the use of force and act of aggression may apply to the use of cyberspace (article 1, para.3) Armed forces of the Islamic Republic of Iran believe that certainly, those cyber operations resulting in material damage to property and/or persons in the widespread and grave manner and or it logically is probable to result in such implications constitutes use of force. Should such operations affect the vital national infrastructures, including defensive infrastructures- whether owned by the public or private sector- they shall violate the principle of the non-use of force. (Article IV, para. 1)","Iran Cyber Statement (2020), Art. 1 Pt. 3 Art. IV, Pt. 1",,
Iran,IRN,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"Armed forces of the Islamic Republic of Iran believe that certainly, those cyber operations resulting in material damage to property and/or persons in the widespread and grave manner and or it logically is probable to result in such implications constitutes use of force. Should such operations affect the vital national infrastructures, including defensive infrastructures- whether owned by the public or private sector- they shall violate the principle of the non-use of force. (Article IV, para. 1)","Iran Cyber Statement (2020),  Art. IV, Pt. 1",,
