Ireland,IRL,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"“At the OEWG, states reaffirmed that “international law, and in particular the Charter of the United Nations, is applicable and essential to maintaining peace and stability and promoting an open, secure, stable, accessible and peaceful ICT environment  The prohibition on the use of force applies regardless of the means or weapons employed.” In Ireland’s view, a cyber-operation attributable to a state will amount to a use of force if its scale and effects correspond to those of a physical use of force.","Ireland (2023) pg. 4, pt. 16, 18",,
Ireland,IRL,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"“The prohibition on the use of force applies regardless of the means or weapons employed."" Although present day technology and our heavily digitised world may not have been contemplated at the time of the adoption of the Charter, it is appropriate to interpret Article 2(4) as applying to force emanating from cyber operations, notwithstanding the fact that the traditional physical or kinetic element may be lacking in terms of both means and impact. (para.18)","Ireland (2023) pg. 4, pt. 17 Ireland (2023) pg. 4, pt. 18",,
Ireland,IRL,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"“In Ireland’s view, a cyber-operation attributable to a state will amount to a use of force if its scale and effects correspond to those of a physical use of force.”  In the Nicaragua case, looking at the notion of “force”, the ICJ determined that assistance to rebels in the form of the provision of weapons or logistical or other support may be regarded as a threat or use of force. The Court further determined that “scale and effects” are to be considered when determining whether particular actions amount to an “armed attack (para. 17)","Ireland (2023) pg. 4, pt. 18 Ireland (2023) pg. 4, pt. 17",,
Ireland,IRL,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"“In Ireland’s view, a cyber-operation attributable to a state will amount to a use of force if its scale and effects correspond to those of a physical use of force. This may include instances where a cyber-operation does not cause physical damage, such as where there is significant impairment of functionality of critical infrastructure.”","Ireland (2023) pg. 4, pt. 18",,
Ireland,IRL,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",No,"“Although present day technology and our heavily digitised world may not have been contemplated at the time of the adoption of the Charter, it is appropriate to interpret Article 2(4) as applying to force emanating from cyber operations, notwithstanding the fact that the traditional physical or kinetic element may be lacking in terms of both means and impact.”","Ireland (2023) pg. 4, pt. 18",,
Ireland,IRL,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,No.,"“This may include instances where a cyber-operation does not cause physical damage, such as where there is significant impairment of functionality of critical infrastructure.”","Ireland (2023) pg. 4, pt. 18",,
Ireland,IRL,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Yes,"“This may include instances where a cyber-operation does not cause physical damage, such as where there is significant impairment of functionality of critical infrastructure.”","Ireland (2023) pg. 4, pt. 18",,
Ireland,IRL,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"“Moreover, not every use of force contrary to Article 2(4) will amount to an “armed attack” within the meaning of Article 51 of the UN Charter...”","Ireland (2023) pg. 4, pt. 19",,
Ireland,IRL,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Israel,ISR,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"the customary prohibition set out in Article 2(4) of the Charter of the United Nations, on “the threat or use of force” in international relations, is clearly applicable in the cyber domain.","Roy Schöndorf, Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations, pg 398 (2020).",,
Israel,ISR,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"| We share the support among States for the view that a cyber operation can amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury or death, which would establish the use of force if caused by kinetic means.","Roy Schöndorf, Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations, pg 398-99 (2020).",,
Israel,ISR,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Ambiguous,"We share the support among States for the view that a cyber operation can amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury or death, which would establish the use of force if caused by kinetic means.",,,
Israel,ISR,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Israel,ISR,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Israel,ISR,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
Israel,ISR,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Ambiguous,"We share the support among States for the view that a cyber operation can amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury or death, which would establish the use of force if caused by kinetic means.","Roy Schöndorf, Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations, pg 398-99 (2020).",,
Israel,ISR,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Ambiguous,"We share the support among States for the view that a cyber operation can amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury or death, which would establish the use of force if caused by kinetic means. For example, hacking into the computers of the railroad network of another State and programming the controls in a manner that is expected to cause a collision between trains can amount to use of force. As with any legal assessment relating to the cyber domain, as practice in this field continues to evolve, there may be room to further examine whether operations not causing physical damage could also amount to use of force.","Roy Schöndorf, Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations, pg 398-99 (2020).",,
Israel,ISR,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"As with any legal assessment relating to the cyber domain, as practice in this field continues to evolve, there may be room to further examine whether operations not causing physical damage could also amount to use of force.","Roy Schöndorf, Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations, pg 399 (2020).",,
Israel,ISR,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Israel,ISR,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Ambiguous,"We share the support among States for the view that a cyber operation can amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury or death, which would establish the use of force if caused by kinetic means. For example, hacking into the computers of the railroad network of another State and programming the controls in a manner that is expected to cause a collision between trains can amount to use of force. As with any legal assessment relating to the cyber domain, as practice in this field continues to evolve, there may be room to further examine whether operations not causing physical damage could also amount to use of force.","Roy Schöndorf, Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations, pg 398-99 (2020).",,
Israel,ISR,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Israel,ISR,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Israel,ISR,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"when the use of force in the cyber domain, by either a State or non-State actor, can be considered as an actual or imminent armed attack, the State under attack may act in accordance with its inherent right to self-defense, as enshrined in Article 51 of the UN Charter. Of course, the exercise of this right is subject to the customary principles of necessity and proportionality.","Roy Schöndorf, Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations, pg 399 (2020).",,
Israel,ISR,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Yes,"First — and this has already been acknowledged by many others— the customary prohibition set out in Article 2(4) of the Charter of the United Nations, on “the threat or use of force” in international relations, is clearly applicable in the cyber domain","Roy Schöndorf, Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations, pg 398-99 (2020).",,
Italy,ITA,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,“There is no established definition or threshold of hostile cyber operations falling within the scope of the prohibition of the ‘use of force’ in the sense of article 2(4) of the UN Charter. Such assessment will be determined on a case-by-case basis depending on the consequences of any given cyber operation.”,"Italian Position Paper, pg.8, § III.a (2021).",,
Italy,ITA,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,“There is no established definition or threshold of hostile cyber operations falling within the scope of the prohibition of the ‘use of force’ in the sense of article 2(4) of the UN Charter. Such assessment will be determined on a case-by-case basis depending on the consequences of any given cyber operation.”,"Italian Position Paper, pg.8, § III.a (2021).",,
Italy,ITA,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Italy,ITA,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Italy,ITA,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"“Italy considers a cyber operation conducted by a State against another State as a use of force, when its scale and effects are comparable to those of a conventional use of force, resulting in physical damage of  property, human injury or loss of life.”","Italian Position Paper, pg.8, § III.a (2021).",,
Italy,ITA,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,“Such assessment will be determined on a case-by-case basis depending on the consequences of any given cyber operation.”,"Italian Position Paper, pg.8, § III.a (2021).",,
Italy,ITA,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,“Such assessment will be determined on a case-by-case basis depending on the consequences of any given cyber operation.”,"Italian Position, Italian Position Paper, pg.8, § III.a (2021).",,
Italy,ITA,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Ambiguous,"“Italy considers a cyber operation conducted by a State against another State as a use of force, when its scale and effects are comparable to those of a conventional use of force, resulting in physical damage of property, human injury or loss of life.” While it is generally accepted that cyber operations resulting in material damage can constitute a use of force, we consider the qualification of cyber operations which merely cause loss of functionality a controversial one. The inclusion of such operations in the scope of the prohibition of the use of force, however, could be justified if one considers that, because of the reliance of modern societies on computers, computer systems and networks, cyber technologies have enabled States to cause the interruption of essential services without the need of physical damage.”","Italian Position Paper, pg.8, § III.a (2021). Italian Position Paper, pg.8, § III.a (2021).",,
Italy,ITA,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Yes,"“Italy considers a cyber operation conducted by a State against another State as a use of force, when its scale and effects are comparable to those of a conventional use of force, resulting in physical damage of property, human injury or loss of life.” While it is generally accepted that cyber operations resulting in material damage can constitute a use of force, we consider the qualification of cyber operations which merely cause loss of functionality a controversial one. The inclusion of such operations in the scope of the prohibition of the use of force, however, could be justified if one considers that, because of the reliance of modern societies on computers, computer systems and networks, cyber technologies have enabled States to cause the interruption of essential services without the need of physical damage.”","Italian Position Paper, pg.8, § III.a (2021). Italian Position Paper, pg.8, § III.a (2021).",,
Italy,ITA,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Italy,ITA,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Ambiguous,"“While it is generally accepted that cyber operations resulting in material damage can constitute a use of force, we consider the qualification of cyber operations which merely cause loss of functionality a controversial one. The inclusion of such operations in the scope of the prohibition of the use of force, however, could be justified if one considers that, because of the reliance of modern societies on computers, computer systems and networks, cyber technologies have enabled States to cause the interruption of essential services without the need of physical damage.”","Italian Position Paper, pg.8, § III.a (2021).",,
Italy,ITA,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
