The Republic of Korea,KOR,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,The Republic of Korea affirms that this principle prohibiting the use of force under international law applies equally to activities conducted in cyberspace.,"National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, § 3.1 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=vxaVEm,
The Republic of Korea,KOR,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"If a certain cyber activity results in physical effects that are equivalent to those caused by a traditional use of force under international law, it may, prima facie, be regarded as a use of force.","National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, § 3.2 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=vxaVEm,
The Republic of Korea,KOR,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
The Republic of Korea,KOR,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
The Republic of Korea,KOR,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Ambigous  Yes,The International Court of Justice (ICJ) has clarified that not every use of force qualifies as an armed attack; only those uses of force that reach a certain threshold of gravity in terms of scale and effects may be considered armed attacks,"National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, § 3.2 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=vxaVEm,
The Republic of Korea,KOR,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,"Whether a cyber activity constitutes a ‘use of force’ must be assessed based on a holistic consideration of its consequences, nature, the identity of the actor, and the target","National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, § 3.2 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=vxaVEm,
The Republic of Korea,KOR,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"If a certain cyber activity results in physical effects that are equivalent to those caused by a traditional use of force… it may, prima facie, be regarded as a use of force.","National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, § 3.2 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=vxaVEm,
The Republic of Korea,KOR,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Yes,…physical effects that are equivalent to those caused by a traditional use of force…,"National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, § 3.2 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=vxaVEm,
