Peru,PER,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"“Based on the foregoing, one might reasonably say that a cyber operation that entails or causes damage of that order of magnitude to military targets or individuals or property protected by international humanitarian law could in itself constitute a use of force.”","Peru, OAS Response, question 3, pg 3 (2019)",,
Peru,PER,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"one might reasonably say that a cyber operation that entails or causes damage of that order of magnitude to military targets or individuals or property protected by international humanitarian law could in itself constitute a use of force. In that sense, the inherent right of states to legitimate self-defense under Article 51 of the United Nations Charter ought to be considered valid. Question 3","Peru, OAS Response, question 3, pg 3 (2019)",,
Peru,PER,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Ambiguous,"With respect to the possibility of a cyber operation that does not cause violent effects being classed as a use of force or an armed attack, it should be noted that if military damage is not caused, the threshold would be met if the act was likely to cause death or injury to persons or property protected from attacks, provided that the military act also satisfies the requirements of direct causation and belligerent nexus","Peru, OAS Response, question 3, pg 3 (2019)",,
Peru,PER,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"Based on the foregoing, one might reasonably say that a cyber operation that entails or causes damage of that order of magnitude to military targets or individuals or property protected by international humanitarian law could in itself constitute a use of force…. With respect to the possibility of a cyber operation that does not cause violent effects being classed as a use of force or an armed attack, it should be noted that if military damage is not caused, the threshold would be met if the act was likely to cause death or injury to persons or property protected from attacks, provided that the military act also satisfies the requirements of direct causation and belligerent nexus","Peru, OAS Response, question 3, pg 3 (2019)",,
Peru,PER,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Ambiguous,"With respect to the possibility of a cyber operation that does not cause violent effects being classed as a use of force or an armed attack, it should be noted that if military damage is not caused, the threshold would be met if the act was likely to cause death or injury to persons or property protected from attacks, provided that the military act also satisfies the requirements of direct causation and belligerent nexus…As to whether a cyber operation can qualify as a use of force or an armed attack without causing violent effects, this General Office is of the opinion that a use of force does not necessarily cause violent effects such that it should be regarded as violating Article 2.4 of the Charter","Peru, OAS Response, question 3, pg 3 (2019)",,
Peru,PER,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,Ambiguous,"cyber operation that entails or causes damage of that order of magnitude to military targets or individuals or property protected by international humanitarian law could in itself constitute a use of force. In that sense, the inherent right of states to legitimate self-defense under Article 51 of the United Nations Charter ought to be considered valid. Question 3","Peru, OAS Response, question 3, pg 3 (2019)",,
Peru,PER,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,In certain circumstances actions in cyberspace may constitute a violation of the prohibition of the use of force.,"The Republic of Poland’s position  on the application of international law in cyberspace, pg. 5, §4 (2022)",,
Poland,POL,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"According to the Advisory Opinion of the International Court of Justice on the legality of the threat or use of nuclear weapons13, an action may be considered the use of force irrespective of the means used. What matters are the effects of the actions taken. As a result, it cannot be ruled out that in some circumstances a cyberattack will reach such a threshold that it will be deemed the use of force.","The Republic of Poland’s position  on the application of international law in cyberspace, pg. 5, §4 (2022)",,
Poland,POL,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Yes,"There is no universally acceptable definition of “coercion”, but an unambiguous example of a prohibited intervention is the use of force.","The Republic of Poland’s position  on the application of international law in cyberspace, pg. 4 (2022)",,
Poland,POL,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,"Perceiving a cyberattack as the use of force is supported by the possibility of it causing similar effects to those caused by a classic armed attack executed with the use of conventional weapons. When assessing whether or not a cyber operation reaches the threshold of the use of force, the situation must be analysed individually, taking into consideration the circumstances of actions taken in accordance with the requirements of international law.","The Republic of Poland’s position  on the application of international law in cyberspace, pg. 5, §4 (2022)",,
Poland,POL,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"Perceiving a cyberattack as the use of force is supported by the possibility of it causing similar effects to those caused by a classic armed attack executed with the use of conventional weapons. When assessing whether or not a cyber operation reaches the threshold of the use of force, the situation must be analysed individually, taking into consideration the circumstances of actions taken in accordance with the requirements of international law.","The Republic of Poland’s position  on the application of international law in cyberspace, pg. 5, §4 (2022)",,
Poland,POL,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,Ambiguous,"Pursuant to Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations and customary international law, a state has the right of self-defence in the event of an armed attack. In the context of cyberspace, a cyberattack that results in death or injury of people or damage or destruction of property of significant value may be considered an armed attack. In such circumstances, according to international law, a state enjoys the right of self-defence, however, this right should be exercised in line with the principles arising from customary international law, namely the principle of necessity and proportionality.","The Republic of Poland’s position  on the application of international law in cyberspace, pg. 5, §5 (2022)",,
Poland,POL,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"The prohibition of the threat or use of force is a well-established principle of international law, being included in art. 2(4) of the UN Charter... In order to ascertain whether a cyber operation represents a threat or use of force and whether it even amounts to a cyberattack, a case-by-case analysis must be carried out...","Romania, UNGA Compendium, pg. 77 (2021)",,
Romania,ROU,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"In order to ascertain whether a cyber operation represents a threat or use of force and whether it even amounts to a cyberattack, a case-by-case analysis must be carried out to determine the circumstance in which the attack occurred, the nature of the operation (military or not) and the scale and the effects of the operation (by comparison against the scale and severity of a conventional (non-cyber) act of violence covered by the prohibition).","Romania, UNGA Compendium, pg. 77 (2021)",,
Romania,ROU,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"In order to ascertain whether a cyber operation represents a threat or use of force and whether it even amounts to a cyberattack, a case-by-case analysis must be carried out to determine the circumstance in which the attack occurred, the nature of the operation (military or not) and the scale and the effects of the operation (by comparison against the scale and severity of a conventional (non-cyber) act of violence covered by the prohibition). The elements of such an analysis, from the “scale and effects” perspective, are well established in the ICJ’s relevant jurisprudence.","Romania, UNGA Compendium, pg. 77 (2021)",,
Romania,ROU,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,"In order to ascertain whether a cyber operation represents a threat or use of force and whether it even amounts to a cyberattack, a case-by-case analysis must be carried out to determine the circumstance in which the attack occurred, the nature of the operation (military or not) and the scale and the effects of the operation (by  comparison against the scale and severity of a conventional (non-cyber) act of violence covered by the prohibition).","Romania, UNGA Compendium, pg. 77 (2021)",,
Romania,ROU,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"In order to ascertain whether a cyber operation represents a threat or use of force and whether it even amounts to a cyberattack, a case-by-case analysis must be carried out to determine the circumstance in which the attack occurred, the nature of the operation (military or not) and the scale and the effects of the operation (by  comparison against the scale and severity of a conventional (non-cyber) act of violence covered by the prohibition).","Romania, UNGA Compendium, pg. 77 (2021)",,
Romania,ROU,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Ambiguous,"In order to ascertain whether a cyber operation represents a threat or use of force and whether it even amounts to a cyberattack, a case-by-case analysis must be carried out to determine the circumstance in which the attack occurred, the nature of the operation (military or not) and the scale and the effects of the operation (by comparison against the scale and severity of a conventional (non-cyber) act of violence covered by the prohibition).","Romania, UNGA Compendium, pg. 77 (2021)",,
Romania,ROU,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"In order to ascertain whether a cyber operation represents a threat or use of force and whether it even amounts to a cyberattack, a case-by-case analysis must be carried out to determine the circumstance in which the attack occurred, the nature of the operation (military or not) and the scale and the effects of the operation (by comparison against the scale and severity of a conventional (non-cyber) act of violence covered by the prohibition).","Romania, UNGA Compendium, pg. 77 (2021)",,
Romania,ROU,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
