Singapore,SGP,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"Singapore affirms that the following key principles enshrined in the UN Charter apply in cyberspace as they do in the physical world, and are of fundamental importance to small States, such as Singapore: ... the obligation of all States to refrain from the threat or use of force against the territorial integrity or political independence of any State. A cyber operation can cause severe consequences and effects.","Singapore, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 83, ¶ 5",,
Singapore,SGP,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Ambiguous,"In determining whether a cyber operation amounts to the use of force, factors that may be taken into account include, but are not limited to, the prevailing circumstances at the time of the cyber operation, the origin of the cyber operation, the effects caused or sought by the cyber operation, the degree of intrusion of the cyber operation, and the nature of the target.",,,
Singapore,SGP,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Ambiguous,"In determining whether a cyber operation amounts to the use of force, factors that may be taken into account include, but are not limited to, the prevailing circumstances at the time of the cyber operation, the origin of the cyber operation, the effects caused or sought by the cyber operation, the degree of intrusion of the cyber operation, and the nature of the target.",,,
Singapore,SGP,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,"“In determining whether a cyber operation amounts to the use of force, factors that may be taken into account include, but are not limited to, the prevailing circumstances at the time of the cyber operation, the origin of the cyber operation, the effects caused or sought by the cyber operation, the degree of intrusion of the cyber operation, and the nature of the target.”","Singapore, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 83, ¶ 5",,
Singapore,SGP,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"“In determining whether a cyber operation amounts to the use of force, factors that may be taken into account include, but are not limited to, the prevailing circumstances at the time of the cyber operation, the origin of the cyber operation, the effects caused or sought by the cyber operation, the degree of intrusion of the cyber operation, and the nature of the target.”","Singapore, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 83, ¶ 5",,
Singapore,SGP,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",No,"In Singapore’s view, it is also possible that, in certain limited circumstances, malicious cyber activity may amount to an armed attack even if it does not necessarily cause death, injury, physical damage or destruction, taking into account the scale and effects of the cyber activity. An example might be a targeted cyber operation causing sustained and long-term outage of Singapore’s critical infrastructure.","Singapore, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 84, ¶8",,
Singapore,SGP,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,No,"In Singapore’s view, it is also possible that, in certain limited circumstances, malicious cyber activity may amount to an armed attack even if it does not necessarily cause death, injury, physical damage or destruction, taking into account the scale and effects of the cyber activity. An example might be a targeted cyber operation causing sustained and long-term outage of Singapore’s critical infrastructure.","Singapore, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 84, ¶8",,
Singapore,SGP,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Yes,"In Singapore’s view, it is also possible that, in certain limited circumstances,   malicious cyber activity may amount to an armed attack even if it does not necessarily   cause death, injury, physical damage or destruction, taking into account the scale and   effects of the cyber activity. An example might be a targeted cyber operation causing sustained and long-term outage of Singapore’s critical infrastructure.","Singapore, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 84, ¶¶8-9",,
Singapore,SGP,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Yes,"A series or combination of cyber-attacks, whether or not it is in combination with kinetic attacks, may amount to an armed attack, even if the individual attacks do not reach the threshold equivalent to an armed attack, as long as the attacks are launched by the same actor or by different attackers acting in concert.","Singapore, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 84, ¶9",,
Singapore,SGP,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Yes,"Singapore affirms that the following key principles enshrined in the UN Charter apply in cyberspace as they do in the physical world, and are of fundamental importance to small States, such as Singapore ... the obligation of all States to refrain from the threat or use of force against the territorial integrity or political independence of any State. A cyber operation can cause severe consequences and effects. In determining whether a cyber operation amounts to the use of force, factors that may be taken into account include, but are not limited to, the prevailing circumstances at the time of the cyber operation, the origin of the cyber operation, the effects caused or sought by the cyber operation, the degree of intrusion of the cyber operation, and the nature of the target.","Singapore, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 83, ¶5",,
Sweden,SWE,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"The prohibition of the use of force is a cardinal rule of customary international law, also applicable in relation to cyber operations.",Sweden Position (July 2021) pg. 3,,
Sweden,SWE,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"The ICJ has declared that the provisions on the use of force are not dependent on the choice of means, but rather apply to any use of force regardless of the weapons employed",Sweden Position (July 2021) pg. 3,,
Sweden,SWE,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Ambiguous,"[quoting Nicaragua for the point] “the ‘element of coercion, which defines, and indeed forms the very essence of, prohibited intervention, is particularly obvious in the case of an intervention which uses force, either in the direct form of military action, or in the indirect form of support for subversive or terrorist armed activities within another State.’”",Sweden Position (July 2021) pg. 3,,
Sweden,SWE,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"The Court has furthermore distinguished the most grave forms of the use of force (those constituting an armed attack) from other less grave forms based on scale and effects. A similar assessment of scale and effects may be made in relation to whether an act constitutes a breach of the prohibition of the use of force. While most cyber operations would not constitute use of force, such operations would be considered as such if comparable to the scale and effects of kinetic use of force. An assessment needs to be made on a case-by-case basis.",Sweden Position (July 2021) pg. 4,,
Sweden,SWE,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,"While most cyber operations would not constitute use of force, such operations would be considered as such if comparable to the scale and effects of kinetic use of force. An assessment needs to be made on a case-by-case basis.",Sweden Position (July 2021) pg. 4,,
Sweden,SWE,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Ambiguous,"While most cyber operations would not constitute use of force, such operations would be considered as such if comparable to the scale and effects of kinetic use of force. An assessment needs to be made on a case-by-case basis.",Sweden Position (July 2021) pg. 4,,
Sweden,SWE,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,,Sweden Position (July 2021) pg. 4,,
Sweden,SWE,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,The Court has furthermore distinguished the most grave forms of the use of force (those constituting an armed attack) from other less grave forms based on scale and effects.,Sweden Position (July 2021) pg. 4,,
Sweden,SWE,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,“The prohibition on the use of force and the right of self-defence are also applicable to cyberspace.”,"Switzerland Annex UN GGE 2019/2021 pg. 4, §4",,
Switzerland,CHE,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,The ICJ has also determined that an armed attack does not necessarily have to involve kinetic military action or the use of weapons because the means by which an attack is perpetrated is not the decisive factor.,"Switzerland Annex UN GGE 2019/2021 pg. 4, §4",,
Switzerland,CHE,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Ambiguous,"“In order to qualify, the scale and effect of the attack must reach a certain threshold of gravity... A state is permitted to exercise its right of self-defence in response to a cyber incident if the incident amounts in scale and effect to that of a kinetic operation in terms of inflicting death or serious injury to persons, or extensive material damage to objects. There are no binding quantitative or qualitative guidelines as to when the threshold of an armed attack in terms of scale and effect has been reached. Current discussions on how to define an armed attack in cyberspace are focusing on attacks on critical infrastructure (e.g. nuclear power plants, power grids) which reach the required threshold in terms of scale and effect i.e. serious injury to persons and/or extensive damage to objects.”","Switzerland Annex UN GGE 2019/2021 pg. 4, §4",,
Switzerland,CHE,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Silence,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",No,Same as previous passage.,"Switzerland Annex UN GGE 2019/2021 pg. 4, §4",,
Switzerland,CHE,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,,"Switzerland Annex UN GGE 2019/2021 pg. 4, §4",,
Switzerland,CHE,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"In accordance with ICJ case law, not every violation of the prohibition on the use of force constitutes an armed attack, but only its gravest form. In order to qualify, the scale and effect of the attack must reach a certain threshold of gravity.","Switzerland Annex UN GGE 2019/2021 pg. 4, §4",,
Switzerland,CHE,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,Thailand reaffirms that Article 2(4) of the Charter of the United Nations… applies fully to cyber operations,"Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, # 20 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=CmZqnO,
Thailand,THA,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"Thailand acknowledges that, in certain circumstances, a cyber operation may amount to a prohibited use of force under international law","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 6, # 21 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=CmZqnO,
Thailand,THA,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Ambiguous,"Cyber operations may amount to a use of force if they cause, or are reasonably expected to cause, physical destruction or injury equivalent in gravity to a kinetic attack. For instance, a cyberattack that disables critical infrastructure—such as power grids, air traffic control systems, or hospital networks—resulting in loss of life, injury, or significant material damage, may meet this threshold",,,
Thailand,THA,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Ambiguous,"ICJ clarified that direct or indirect coercive measures, including the threat or use of force, may constitute unlawful intervention.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, # 16 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=CmZqnO,
Thailand,THA,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Yes,"Thailand acknowledges that, in certain circumstances, a cyber operation may amount to a prohibited use of force under international law. This determination must be based on an assessment of the scale and effects of the cyber operation, including its foreseeable consequences on human life, property, or loss of functionality of critical infrastructure","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 6, # 21 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=CmZqnO,
Thailand,THA,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"Cyber operations may amount to a use of force if they cause, or are reasonably expected to cause, physical destruction or injury equivalent in gravity to a kinetic attack. For instance, a cyberattack that disables critical infrastructure—such as power grids, air traffic control systems, or hospital networks—resulting in loss of life, injury, or significant material damage, may meet this threshold","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 6, # 22 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=CmZqnO,
Thailand,THA,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Yes,"Cyber operations may amount to a use of force if they cause, or are reasonably expected to cause, physical destruction or injury equivalent in gravity to a kinetic attack","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 6, # 22 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=CmZqnO,
Thailand,THA,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"Cyber operations may amount to a use of force if they cause, or are reasonably expected to cause, physical destruction or injury equivalent in gravity to a kinetic attack","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 6, # 22 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=CmZqnO,
Thailand,THA,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimus effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",No,"“…if they cause, or are reasonably expected to cause, physical destruction or injury…” ""Disruption to essential State functions, absent accompanying physical destruction... does not by itself amount to an armed attack.""","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 6, # 22 (2025) Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 7, # 26 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=CmZqnO,
Thailand,THA,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,No,"The distinction... between armed attacks and other less grave forms of use of force should be assessed. Thailand affirms that a malicious cyber operation attributable to a State that causes death, injury, physical damage or destruction equivalent to a traditional non-cyber armed attack, would constitute an armed attack","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 6, # 25 (2025)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=CmZqnO,
Thailand,THA,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"I’m focusing on the law applicable in peacetime because the UK has already set out that cyber operations are capable of breaching the prohibition on the threat or use of force, and that the law applicable in armed conflict applies just the same to the use of cyber means as other means of waging war. The Charter of the United Nations applies to States’ conduct in cyberspace, as it does to their other conduct.   Article 2(4) of the UN Charter prohibits the threat or use of force against the territorial integrity or political independence of any State or in any other manner inconsistent with the purposes of the United Nations. Depending on the facts and circumstances in each case, conduct by States carried out in cyberspace is capable of constituting a threat or use of force if the actual or threatened conduct has or would have the same or similar effects of conduct using kinetic means.","International Law in Future Frontiers, Chatham House (2022) pg. 5 UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 115, ¶4 Application of International Law to States’ Conduct in Cyberspace: UK Statement, Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (2021), ¶5",,
United Kingdom,GBR,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"Depending on the facts and circumstances in each case, conduct by States carried out in cyberspace is capable of constituting a threat or use of force if the actual or threatened conduct has or would have the same or similar effects of conduct using kinetic means. The circumstances in which the threat or use of force is not unlawful under international law are the same irrespective of whether the conduct is by kinetic or cyber means.","Application of International Law to States’ Conduct in Cyberspace: UK Statement, Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (2021), ¶5",,
United Kingdom,GBR,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Ambiguous,"Depending on the facts and circumstances in each case, conduct by States carried out in cyberspace is capable of constituting a threat or use of force if the actual or threatened conduct has or would have the same or similar effects of conduct using kinetic means. The circumstances in which the threat or use of force is not unlawful under international law are the same irrespective of whether the conduct is by kinetic or cyber means.","Application of International Law to States’ Conduct in Cyberspace: UK Statement, Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (2021), ¶5",,
United Kingdom,GBR,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"Depending on the facts and circumstances in each case, conduct by States carried out in cyberspace is capable of constituting a threat or use of force if the actual or threatened conduct has or would have the same or similar effects of conduct using kinetic means. The circumstances in which the threat or use of force is not unlawful under international law are the same irrespective of whether the conduct is by kinetic or cyber means. (para.5)","Application of International Law to States’ Conduct in Cyberspace: UK Statement, Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (2021), ¶5",,
United Kingdom,GBR,Does the State recognize a “small” or de minimis effects exception to the prohibition on the use of force?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,"Can non-violent effects alone qualify as “force” (e.g., substantial economic harm, losses of functionality, etc.)?",Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,"Can spill over effects from a cyber operation (i.e., those that are secondary or knock-on from the immediate effects) qualify as a use of force?",Yes,"If a hostile state interferes with the operation of one of our nuclear reactors, resulting in widespread loss of life, the fact that the act is carried out by way of a cyber operation does not prevent it from being viewed as an unlawful use of force or an armed attack against us. If it would be a breach of international law to bomb an air traffic control tower with the effect of downing civilian aircraft, then it will be a breach of international law to use a hostile cyber operation to disable air traffic control systems which results in the same, ultimately lethal effects.","Cyber and International Law in the 21st Century (2018), pg.6 ¶2  United Kingdom, International Law in Future Frontiers (2022), pg.8 ¶4",,
United Kingdom,GBR,Does the State recognize an accumulation theory or similar aggregation theory of use of force?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,Does the State consider a use of force to be the equivalent of an armed attack (per Article 51 of the UN Charter) subject to the same threshold?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,Can a cyber operation amount to a prohibited threat of a use of force?,Yes,"Depending on the facts and circumstances in each case, conduct by States carried out in cyberspace is capable of constituting a threat or use of force if the actual or threatened conduct has or would have the same or similar effects of conduct using kinetic means.","Application of International Law to States’ Conduct in Cyberspace: UK Statement, Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (2021), ¶5",,
United States,USA,Does the prohibition of use of force apply to cyber operations?,Yes,"Depending on the circumstances, a military cyber operation may constitute a use of force within the meaning of Article 2(4) of the U.N. Charter and Customary International Law. Cyber activities may in certain circumstances constitute uses of force within the meaning of Article 2(4) of the UN Charter and customary international law.","DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Commanda Legal Conference United States, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 137, II(A)(1)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20States/United%20States_2020%20Speech_DOD%20General%20Counsel%20Remarks%20at%20U.S.%20Cyber%20Command%20Legal%20Conference.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=L0uVR1,
United States,USA,Can a stand-alone cyber operation violate the prohibition on the use of force?,Yes,"Although this is necessarily a case-by-case, fact-specific inquiry, cyber activities that proximately result in death, injury, or significant destruction, or represent an imminent threat thereof, would likely be viewed as a use of force / armed attack. If the physical consequences of a cyber activity result in the kind of damage that dropping a bomb or firing a missile would, that cyber activity should equally be considered a use of force / armed attack.","United States, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 137, II(A)(1)",,
United States,USA,Must a State intend for a cyber operation to amount to a use of force for there to be a violation of the prohibition on the use of force?,Ambiguous,"Some of the factors States should evaluate in assessing whether an event constitutes an actual or imminent use of force / armed attack in or through cyberspace include the context of the event, the actor perpetrating the action (recognizing the challenge of attribution in cyberspace, including the ability of an attacker to masquerade as another person/entity or manipulate transmission data to make it appear as if the cyber activity was launched from a different location or by a different person), the target and its location, the effects of the cyber activity, and the intent of the actor (recognizing that intent, like the identity of the attacker, may be difficult to discern, but that hostile intent may be inferred from the particular circumstances of a cyber activity), among other factors.","UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 137, II(A)(1)",,
United States,USA,Must the cyber operation have a coercive character (or effect) to qualify as a use of force?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,Does the State apply a “scale and effects” test to assess whether an action constitutes a use of force?,No,"Some of the factors States should evaluate in assessing whether an event constitutes an actual or imminent use of force / armed attack in or through cyberspace include the context of the event, the actor perpetrating the action (recognizing the challenge of attribution in cyberspace, including the ability of an attacker to masquerade as another person/entity or manipulate transmission data to make it appear as if the cyber activity was launched from a different location or by a different person), the target and its location, the effects of the cyber activity, and the intent of the actor (recognizing that intent, like the identity of the attacker, may be difficult to discern, but that hostile intent may be inferred from the particular circumstances of a cyber activity), among other factors.","United States, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 137, II(A)(1)",,
United States,USA,Does the State apply a totality of circumstances or similar context-based test to assess uses of force?,Yes,"States should consider the nature and extent of injury or death to persons and the destruction of, or damage to, property. Although this is necessarily a case-by-case, fact-specific inquiry, cyber activities that proximately result in death, injury, or significant destruction, or represent an imminent threat thereof, would likely be viewed as a use of force / armed attack. If the physical consequences of a cyber activity result in the kind of damage that dropping a bomb or firing a missile would, that cyber activity should equally be considered a use of force / armed attack.  Some of the factors States should evaluate in assessing whether an event constitutes an actual or imminent use of force / armed attack in or through cyberspace include the context of the event, the actor perpetrating the action (recognizing the challenge of attribution in cyberspace, including the ability of an attacker to masquerade as another person/entity or manipulate transmission data to make it appear as if the cyber activity was launched from a different location or by a different person), the target and its location, the effects of the cyber activity, and the intent of the actor (recognizing that intent, like the identity of the attacker, may be difficult to discern, but that hostile intent may be inferred from the particular circumstances of a cyber activity), among other factors.","United States, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 137, II(A)(1)",,
United States,USA,Does the State weigh the actual consequences of an action to assess whether it amounts to a use of force?,Yes,"States should consider the nature and extent of injury or death to persons and the destruction of, or damage to, property. Although this is necessarily a case-by-case, fact-specific inquiry, cyber activities that proximately result in death, injury, or significant destruction, or represent an imminent threat thereof, would likely be viewed as a use of force / armed attack. If the physical consequences of a cyber activity result in the kind of damage that dropping a bomb or firing a missile would, that cyber activity should equally be considered a use of force / armed attack.","United States, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 137, II(A)(1)",,
United States,USA,"Does the State only recognize a use of force in cyber contexts by analogy to consequences caused by kinetic force (e.g., injury, death, damage or destruction)?",Ambiguous,"States should consider the nature and extent of injury or death to persons and the destruction of, or damage to, property. Although this is necessarily a case-by-case, fact-specific inquiry, cyber activities that proximately result in death, injury, or significant destruction, or represent an imminent threat thereof, would likely be viewed as a use of force / armed attack. If the physical consequences of a cyber activity result in the kind of damage that dropping a bomb or firing a missile would, that cyber activity should equally be considered a use of force / armed attack.","United States, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 137, II(A)(1)",,
United States,USA,Are kinetic-like effects a required consequence of a cyber operation to qualify as a use of force (versus a factor among others to be considered)?,Ambiguous,"States should consider the nature and extent of injury or death to persons and the destruction of, or damage to, property. Although this is necessarily a case-by-case, fact-specific inquiry, cyber activities that proximately result in death, injury, or significant destruction, or represent an imminent threat thereof, would likely be viewed as a use of force / armed attack. If the physical consequences of a cyber activity result in the kind of damage that dropping a bomb or firing a missile would, that cyber activity should equally be considered a use of force / armed attack. Some of the factors States should evaluate in assessing whether an event constitutes an actual or imminent use of force / armed attack in or through cyberspace include the context of the event, the actor perpetrating the action (recognizing the challenge of attribution in cyberspace, including the ability of an attacker to masquerade as another person/entity or manipulate transmission data to make it appear as if the cyber activity was launched from a different location or by a different person), the target and its location, the effects of the cyber activity.","United States, UNGA Compendium (2021) pg. 137, II(A)(1)",,
